    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini.diff\r\n\r\n### Description\r\n\r\nWhen opening Neovim under some specific conditions which I can’t figure out, the following error message appears, possibly multiple times:\r\n\r\n```\r\nError executing vim.schedule lua callback: BufWinEnter Autocommands for \"*\"..function <SNR>30_Highlight_Matching_Pair, line 149: Vim(call):E801: ID already taken: 3                                                                                                            \r\nstack traceback:                                                                                                                        \r\n        [C]: in function 'bufload'                                                                                                      \r\n        /home/xigoi/.local/share/nvim/lazy/mini/lua/mini/diff.lua:438: in function 'enable'                                             \r\n        /home/xigoi/.local/share/nvim/lazy/mini/lua/mini/diff.lua:989: in function ''                                                   \r\n        vim/_editor.lua: in function <vim/_editor.lua:0>\r\n```\r\n\r\nObservations:\r\n\r\n* I have only observed this happening with files written in the [xidoc](https://xidoc.nim.town/) language. **However**, what’s truly weird is that the filetype does not actually matter, only the contents. If I take a xidoc file and rename it to a different filetype, the error still occurs. If I take a non-xidoc file and give it the .xd extension, the error does not occur.\r\n* The error only seems to occur when opening at least 3 files at once, and opening more files generally increases the number of errors, but in a non-deterministic way.\r\n* The errors don’t show up instantly; there is a small delay between them.\r\n* Commenting out line 438 in mini/diff.lua seems to fix the issue, though it obviously breaks the plugin.\r\n\r\n### Neovim version\r\n\r\n0.9.4\r\n\r\n### Steps to reproduce\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, as mentioned, I don’t know how to reliably create this issue. I can send some files for which it happens if needed.\r\n\r\nThe `mini.diff` setup is irrelevant, the issue still happens with an empty setup.\r\n\r\n### Expected behavior\r\n\r\nNo errors\r\n\r\n### Actual behavior\r\n\r\nThe mentioned errors appear.",
